{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793653732,"nonce":"b2Hy8ITjGILGR+5qckxPtVhrwZA=","title":"Plan a CN and blocks component catalog on blinc.rs with embedded WASM demos","message":"Owner request, 2026-11-02: enrich the Blinc website with a component catalog and per-component pages, inspired by Tailwind CSS documentation and Affect UI, with live embedded WASM demos and documentation covering both blinc_cn and the planned blocks library. This planning work is separate from the cargo release goal and links to the blocks plan 5ce78232a18280148736276979dbaeda0b8edd340c8eaf1ac20cacad1d2cdf07. No website/crate source edits, builds, commits or deployment were performed for this plan.\n\nExisting infrastructure to reuse\n- web/ is the static landing site, published at blinc.rs; docs/book/ is mdBook under /docs/.\n- .github/workflows/docs.yml generates/builds WASM wrappers, builds mdBook, stages demos under target/site/docs/examples/, copies web/ into target/site/, then deploys the combined Pages artifact.\n- tools/build-web-examples discovers public build_ui examples, generates wasm wrapper crates and iframe hosts, and produces gallery Markdown. docs/book/src/web/example-gallery/cn_demo.md already embeds a live CN gallery.\n- Reuse wrapper/bootstrap and static staging machinery, while giving each component a focused demo. Audit the current capability/failure messages: the generated host checks both WebGPU and WebGL2, but its fallback copy mentions WebGPU only, and presence of navigator.gpu alone does not establish successful adapter/device initialization.\n- Current public site and old examples describe older releases. Component pages must identify the API/demo version rather than silently pairing new source with an older install command.\n\nInformation architecture\n- /components/ is a searchable catalog, with CN and Blocks sections plus categories, tags, maturity and web-capability metadata. Use static screenshots/thumbnails for index cards; each card links to its detail page.\n- Canonical detail routes: /components/cn/\u003cslug\u003e/ and /components/blocks/\u003cslug\u003e/. A family page can document related helper parts (for example card/header/content/footer), with explicit coverage mapping for public component families.\n- Static rendered HTML provides navigation, documentation, source panels and search metadata; the actual component preview is Blinc compiled to WASM. This lets readers use the documentation independently of the GPU preview.\n- Keep conceptual guides in mdBook and link to the canonical component reference. Share content/snippets where needed and redirect old reference URLs when moved, preserving anchors rather than maintaining two diverging API references.\n- Components and blocks share the page template and metadata schema. Blocks enter the public live catalog as they are implemented; planned items carry an explicit maturity label rather than a broken or simulated preview.\n\nRequired component-page contents\n1. Purpose, library/version, maturity and platform capability notes.\n2. Live preview with controls appropriate to the component, variant selection, light/dark theme, viewport widths, reset and open-standalone actions. Only one preview instance runs at a time on a detail page; an example selector switches the active demo.\n3. Copyable Rust usage and installation/setup instructions, including the required theme bundle/stylesheet registration. Examples use Signal, Computed and Effect and the prop-binding APIs.\n4. Runtime props versus structural configuration, defaults, controlled values, typed callback/event payloads and custom-content slots.\n5. Restyling reference: root/part/modifier classes, state attributes and values, pseudo-classes, CSS custom properties and theme tokens, slot boundaries, and any separately mounted overlay roots. Include default and consumer-override CSS with hover/focus/disabled/loading examples where relevant.\n6. Keyboard and pointer interaction, focus behavior, constrained-width/long-content examples, and an honest accessibility/platform support description.\n7. Related atoms/blocks, source link pinned to the built commit, version/migration notes, and a link to complete runnable source.\n\nSingle source of truth for docs, source and demo\n- Introduce a shared catalog manifest containing library, stable slug, category/tags, release/status, canonical documentation source, public API/restyling metadata, example IDs, Rust/CSS source locations, demo bundle/entry, viewport defaults and capability requirements.\n- Generate catalog navigation/search/index pages and demo wiring from this manifest. Check that every listed available component has docs and at least one working minimal demo.\n- Usage snippets come from the same compile-checked Rust example source used by native and WASM demo fixtures. CSS panels reference the actual example stylesheet. Hand-authored behavior and API descriptions remain reviewed content, with selector/entry/link validation to catch drift.\n- Publish a build manifest tying page metadata, snippets, JS/WASM/assets, lockfile/features and source revision together. Stable documentation tracks published crate APIs; unreleased demonstrations are explicitly labelled next/preview.\n- Adapt existing web-example tooling through small shared modules rather than creating a parallel discovery/build system or broad refactor of the existing generator. Reuse the current static deployment architecture; a new server-rendered application or framework migration is not required for the catalog.\n\nWASM demo architecture\n- Use focused demo modules registered by component/example ID. Start with one small pilot bundle, then measure whether forms, overlays, data and blocks should have separate bundles. Avoid automatically creating one complete WASM package per variant or loading the entire CN gallery for every small widget.\n- Host a live demo in a same-origin iframe, with its own WASM application, Blinc context, theme and canvas. Reuse the current iframe model instead of depending on multiple independent Blinc applications sharing one browser-global runtime.\n- A typed versioned host/demo bridge supports ready/error, selected example, allowed prop controls, theme, CSS apply/reset and reset/resize events. Demo controls write Signal values or the existing prop-binding/editor handles; Computed derives semantic data and Effect handles owned subscriptions. Structural example/variant switches remount deliberately when necessary.\n- The component inside the preview is the real CN/block implementation. JavaScript supplies host controls and the bridge, rather than drawing a lookalike component or duplicating the widget's behavior.\n- Define loading, ready, failed, suspended and restarting states for the host. Capture bootstrap/WASM/adapter/device/asset failures and show a retryable explanation with docs/source and a static preview still available. WebGL2 capability is described per demo, not assumed to match WebGPU for every effect.\n- Size the canvas from the actual available viewport and device pixel ratio. Include narrow/wide modes and verify overlay menus, dialogs, sheets, tooltips and toast exits stay usable within the frame; open-standalone is available for large compositions. Existing web runner listens to window.resize, so audit element resizing/ResizeObserver needs before relying on auto-height layouts.\n\nLive CSS restyling\n- CN/block class names describe nodes in Blinc's canvas-rendered tree. Styling the website DOM or iframe element does not apply those rules to the component. Restyling presets and any CSS editor must pass CSS into the WASM app's Blinc stylesheet engine.\n- Use a replaceable scoped preview stylesheet over the normal CN/block styles, with apply/reset and parse errors. WindowedContext::add_css currently appends both raw sources and merged rules, so repeated editor updates must not accumulate stale rules or sources indefinitely. Design a supported replacement layer or reset/remount strategy; do not repurpose the doc-hidden hot-reload reset as a general widget-editing API.\n- Initial release can use a small set of documented CSS presets before adding freeform CSS editing. Both use the same engine and should demonstrate that documented classes, variables and interactive-state selectors actually work.\n- Rust source is copyable and precompiled in the initial catalog. A browser Rust compiler/remote playground is a separate future project; editing CSS and prop controls does not require one.\n\nPerformance and lifecycle\n- The catalog index downloads static previews; detail pages initialize the selected demo only near view or after activation. Set a bounded number of active GPU contexts and frames.\n- Parent visibility/intersection and document visibility jointly control suspension or teardown. CSS-hiding an iframe is not an adequate lifecycle implementation. Audit the current self-perpetuating frame loop and event-listener ownership before claiming full pause/dispose support. Until a supported pause API exists, host-managed iframe unloading/remounting is an explicit fallback, with memory and resource behavior measured.\n- Switching examples/navigating away releases the prior instance and its subscriptions, listeners, timers and retained assets. Preserve/reset demo inputs according to an explicit host policy. Avoid overlapping old/new instances during rapid example changes.\n- Cache content-addressed bundles and shared fonts/assets, with loading progress/failure recovery. Measure compressed transfer, initialization/first-frame latency, retained memory, live context count and idle/hidden frame requests before choosing bundle boundaries or making performance claims.\n\nBuild, deployment and quality\n- Extend the existing Pages artifact layout with generated components/ pages and namespaced demo assets. Staging happens after generators that wipe output directories. Keep the existing landing/book/gallery routes working.\n- Local and PR checks select the affected page and WASM bundle. Reuse Cargo dependency caches; asset-output caches require complete dependency/toolchain/lockfile/feature/source fingerprints, particularly CSS/shaders/fonts/assets. The existing docs workflow explicitly removed stale pkg caching, so avoid reintroducing that failure mode. Ship page and demo artifact references atomically.\n- Documentation-only edits should not require rebuilding every unrelated WASM example. A full catalog integrity/build check belongs to publication validation. Use CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 for Cargo invocations; no builds were run for this planning task.\n- Validate registry entries, canonical links/deep links, source regions, selectors, version/install metadata and compiled example snippets. Per-page browser smoke checks assert readiness, real interaction, theme/prop/CSS updates, reset, viewport resize, focus/keyboard behavior, bootstrap failure and navigation teardown. Pair browser tests with existing native recorder tests; visually inspect generated previews and snapshots.\n- Exercise supported WebGPU and fallback paths in appropriate browser/CI environments. Report actual browser/capability results; do not treat a successful wasm build or navigator.gpu presence as a complete browser rendering pass. Keep documentation navigation and source copy accessible independently of the canvas's current platform-semantic support.\n\nPhased delivery\nA. Shared manifest, static catalog/detail template, demo host/bootstrap, exact-source snippets and lifecycle/capability spike. Pilot button, input, select, dialog, progress and one chart, covering pointer/keyboard/text editing, overlays, animation, CSS restyling and canvas drawing. Use the pilot to settle bundle size and resize/teardown contracts.\nB. Expand to all implemented CN component families, including state/variant examples, searchable navigation, class/variable references and consistent screenshots. Fill measured browser/atom gaps in the relevant shared layers.\nC. Add each blocks component alongside its implementation from plan5ce7823, reusing the same documentation, source, demo and restyling contract. Each block is complete only when its reference page and runnable example exist.\nD. Optional enhancements: freeform CSS editor, shareable demo-control URLs and richer prop inspectors. Rust compilation/playground and whole-application screen galleries remain separate extensions.\n\nDependencies and coordination\n- 5ce78232a18280148736276979dbaeda0b8edd340c8eaf1ac20cacad1d2cdf07: blocks library plan and mandatory CSS-first/prop-binding/restyling-doc contract.\n- 57c4c44b30e5ee203455e03d6e165788256e3c7c627b321eaed83c17aec5ed1a: existing book examples name absent APIs; the catalog must compile its exact examples.\n- d58924b: existing open web toast-exit motion issue, relevant to the overlay pilot. Recheck status before implementation.\n- Current field binding and CSS/region/chart gaps listed in the handoff/blocks plan apply to the affected demos. Page availability follows working feature support; the whole catalog is not a new cargo-release blocker.\n- Source was inspected read-only while current CN/editor work continued in the shared worktree. Recheck APIs/status before implementation; do not modify the ongoing diagnostic or unrelated work.\n\nReference material reviewed 2026-11-02:\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components\nhttps://tailwindcss.com/docs/hover-focus-and-other-states\nhttps://blinc.rs/\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/visibilitychange_event\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGPU_API\nLocal anchors: .github/workflows/docs.yml, docs/book/book.toml, docs/book/src/web/example-gallery/cn_demo.md, tools/build-web-examples/src/main.rs, crates/blinc_app/src/web.rs and windowed.rs.","files":null}]}